# .identity-allow — commit identities this repository accepts.
#
# Copy to `.identity-allow` and COMMIT it. Unlike `.privacy-patterns` (which is
# per-clone and gitignored), this file is a property of the repository: every
# clone should agree on who is allowed to author its commits.
#
# The guard blocks a commit whose author OR committer address is not listed
# here. GitHub no-reply addresses are always accepted and need no entry:
#
#     *@users.noreply.github.com
#
# so most repositories never need this file at all.
#
# Why the default is a no-reply address: author and committer metadata publish
# with every commit and cannot be corrected afterwards without rewriting
# history and force-pushing. A no-reply address attributes the commit to the
# account without publishing a mailbox, which keeps that mistake unavailable
# rather than merely discouraged.
#
# One entry per line. An entry is either a full address or a `*@domain` glob.
# Matching is case-insensitive. Comments and blank lines are ignored.
#
# Declare an identity here when the repository genuinely commits under it —
# for example a project that signs its work with a team domain, or one that
# accepts contributions from a build account. Do not add an address merely to
# silence the guard on a machine that is misconfigured; fix the machine:
#
#     git config --global user.email '<ID>+<user>@users.noreply.github.com'
#
# (The address is shown at github.com/settings/emails under "Keep my email
# address private". Check `git config --local user.email` too — a per-repo
# override beats the global one and is easy to forget.)

# *@example-team.org
# releases@example-team.org
